In Apache Airflow, if an Operator is the person flipping the switch, 
the Hook is the hidden electrical wiring connecting that switch to the power grid. 
They work in tandem, but you cannot build secure, extensible pipelines without understanding 
the strict boundary between them.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Operators: An Operator represents a single, logical task in your DAG 
(like executing a SQL script or transferring a file). 
It is the 'What'. When you place a SnowflakeOperator in your DAG,
you are telling Airflow what you want to achieve.

Hooks: A Hook is the low-level interface that handles the 'How'.
The SnowflakeOperator actually has no idea how to talk to Snowflake over the network. 
Under the hood, it instantiates a SnowflakeHook. 
The Hook manages the messy mechanics: it retrieves the connection credentials,
establishes the TCP session, handles the API request, and manages the teardown.

Buisness Context :- 
This separation of concerns is a massive win for enterprise security.
Because Hooks integrate directly with Airflow's Connection metadata—which we typically back with a 
secure vault like AWS Secrets Manager or HashiCorp Vault—we can automatically 
rotate database passwords or API keys without touching a single line of pipeline code.
It ensures strict Infosec compliance by keeping secrets entirely out of the DAG files.

Design Decision :- 
When data engineers need to pull data from a niche, proprietary internal API, 
they usually take the easy way out: they write a massive, ugly PythonOperator filled with raw requests.get() calls,
hardcoded tokens, and inline retry loops. As a data engineer, I reject that in code review. 
I will build a custom internal Hook to encapsulate the API's unique authentication and pagination logic,
and then build a custom Operator on top of it. 
This keeps the actual DAG file declarative, clean, and incredibly easy to unit test using mocked Hook connections.
